Cross-seed comparison reveals the limits of $K_{\text{eff}}$ as a predictor. Mistral-7B's three FOLIO seeds produce $\kappa = 0.50$, $0.41$, $0.40$ ($K_{\text{eff}} = 1.85$, $2.17$, $2.22$), yet $\Delta$ is non-monotonic: the seed with the lowest $K_{\text{eff}}$ (s42, $1.85$) produces the largest gain ($+4.90$\,pp), while s456 ($K_{\text{eff}} = 2.22$) shows $\Delta = -0.49$\,pp. This non-monotonicity indicates that seed-level sampling variance, not trace independence alone, determines whether the constraint pool happens to contain discriminative information. Two conditions are jointly necessary: sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ (the Qwen conditions with $K_{\text{eff}} \leq 1.31$ never show positive $\Delta$) and sufficient SC accuracy (exp-046 with the highest $K_{\text{eff}} = 3.63$ but SC $= 39\%$ produces significant harm). The SC plateau remains explained by the correlation floor: beyond $K_{\text{eff}}$, additional traces are redundant.

\subsection{Extraction Error Taxonomy}
\label{sec:extraction-taxonomy}

Constraints must discriminate between candidate answers for MAX-SAT to produce a ranking different from majority vote. We measure discriminative power at two levels on Qwen2.5-14B across FOLIO and ProofWriter ($n = 804$). At the source-trace level, 39.2\% of total constraint weight originates from traces whose answer differs from the plurality, but only 12.1\% of individual constraints fall into this minority category. At the Z3 semantic level, the picture is worse: only 8.2\% of constraints [95\% CI: 7.6, 8.8] are discriminative, producing different satisfiability outcomes when evaluated against different candidate answers.

The primary mechanism behind this collapse is variable naming inconsistency. Each trace produces constraints with its own variable names, so Z3 treats identically-intended propositions as unrelated symbols. Among cross-answer constraint pairs, 56.4\% share zero Z3 variables, and the solver cannot detect logical relationships when constraints use disjoint symbol sets. Even when we filter the constraint pool to retain only the 8.2\% discriminative subset, accuracy does not improve. The discriminative constraints are too sparse and too weakly correlated with correctness to shift the MAX-SAT solution. The non-discriminative majority does not merely dilute the signal; the discriminative minority itself lacks the coherence to overcome noise.

\subsection{K Sensitivity}
\label{sec:k-sensitivity}

Figure~\ref{fig:k-sensitivity} shows SICA $\Delta$ as a function of the number of sampled traces $K$ for Mistral-7B on FOLIO ($n = 204$, seed 42). The relationship follows an inverted-U curve: $K = 4$ yields $\Delta = -1.47$\,pp, $K = 8$ yields $\Delta = -0.98$\,pp, $K = 12$ reaches the peak at $\Delta = +4.90$\,pp, and $K = 16$ drops to $\Delta = +1.47$\,pp. At low $K$, the constraint pool lacks diversity and MAX-SAT has too few clauses to produce a ranking different from SC. At high $K$, diminishing returns from non-independent traces (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) add redundant constraints without new discriminative information. The $K = 12$ peak reflects a transient balance between constraint diversity and trace independence, but even this optimum captures only 34.5\% of the oracle ceiling ($+14.22$\,pp). The narrow peak further constrains practical applicability: the best $K$ depends on the model-dataset condition and cannot be determined without labeled data.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{f3_k_sensitivity}
\caption{SICA $\Delta$ (pp) vs.\ number of traces $K$ for Mistral-7B on FOLIO ($n = 204$, seed 42, $T = 0.7$). The inverted-U curve peaks at $K = 12$. Error bars: 95\% bootstrap CI.}
\label{fig:k-sensitivity}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Temperature Sensitivity}
\label{sec:t-sensitivity}

Raising the sampling temperature from $T = 0.7$ to $T = 1.0$ on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) collapses the SICA gain from $\Delta = +4.90$\,pp to $\Delta = +0.49$\,pp (SC $= 54.9\%$, SICA $= 55.4\%$, $p = 0.739$). Higher temperature increases output diversity but degrades trace quality. The net effect is that noisier traces produce noisier formalizations, and the quality loss outweighs any independence gain.

\subsection{Entropy-Conditioned Mechanism}
\label{sec:entropy}

Per-problem answer entropy (computed from the $K = 12$ trace answer distribution) reveals that SICA's aggregate $\Delta$ is driven by a specific subpopulation. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, the relationship is monotonic: low-entropy problems show $\Delta = 0$\,pp, medium-entropy problems show $\Delta \approx +4.5$\,pp, and high-entropy problems show $\Delta = +9.6$\,pp (full breakdown in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:entropy_conditioned}). SICA helps most where traces disagree most. The proportion of high-entropy problems determines the aggregate effect. Mistral-7B FOLIO has 35.8\% high-entropy problems, and the aggregate $\Delta = +4.90$\,pp. Qwen2.5-14B FOLIO has only 10.3\% high-entropy problems; SICA achieves $\Delta = +4.8$\,pp within that subgroup, but the aggregate $\Delta = -0.49$\,pp because the subpopulation is too small to shift overall accuracy. Qwen2.5-14B on LogiQA represents the extreme case: only 8.0\% of problems are high-entropy (75.0\% are low-entropy), and SICA produces $\Delta = +0.0$\,pp even within the high-entropy bin.

This pattern explains the model-strength dependence observed in \S\ref{sec:main-results}. Stronger models produce more consistent traces, which compresses answer entropy, which shrinks the subpopulation where SICA can operate. The causal chain (strong model $\to$ consistent traces $\to$ low entropy $\to$ redundant constraints $\to$ no gain) bounds SICA's ceiling for high-accuracy models independent of extraction quality.

\subsection{Constraint Ablation}
\label{sec:ablation}

To test whether the Z3 solver contributes beyond simple vote counting, we compare four scoring methods on Qwen2.5-14B across FOLIO and ProofWriter ($n = 804$). MAX-SAT achieves 76.49\% (615/804). Count-only (sum constraint weights per candidate without invoking Z3) achieves 76.62\% (616/804). Random-weight MAX-SAT achieves 75.87\% (610/804). The SC baseline achieves 76.24\% (613/804). MAX-SAT and count-only agree on 803 of 804 predictions. The Z3 solver adds nothing over simple counting. This confirms the degeneration in Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}: when only ${\sim}8$\% of constraints are semantically discriminative (\S\ref{sec:extraction-taxonomy}), the MAX-SAT objective reduces to a weighted vote count and SICA collapses to SC.

\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

We test nine remediation strategies spanning six intervention dimensions (full results in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}). \emph{Prompting}: contrastive prompting reduces the discriminative rate from 8.2\% to 6.0\% ($\Delta = -1.1$\,pp), and filtering to retain only discriminative constraints yields zero gain. \emph{Architecture}: cross-model extraction produces 0\% discriminative constraints ($\Delta = 0$\,pp). \emph{Capability}: generational scaling with Qwen3-14B yields $\Delta = +0.49$\,pp (n.s.). \emph{Verification}: Z3 feedback loops trigger UNSAT on only 0.9\% of problems with no real accuracy impact; cross-trace UNSAT checking finds 56.4\% of constraint pairs share zero variables, so logical verification is vacuous. \emph{Diversity}: raising temperature to $T = 1.0$ collapses $\Delta$ from $+4.90$\,pp to $+0.49$\,pp (\S\ref{sec:t-sensitivity}); multi-temperature sampling ($n = 204$) produces $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp (SC $= 57.84\%$, SICA $= 57.84\%$). \emph{Filtering}: consistency filtering (retaining constraints in $\geq 4$ traces) prunes aggressively (1.8 constraints per problem at threshold 4, 0.3 at threshold 8) and reverts to SC. All six dimensions fail. The independence bottleneck is structural: it cannot be resolved by prompt engineering, solver refinements, or diversity manipulations within the same-model self-extraction paradigm.

